 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'ein ovsin' mean?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav Yehuda's definition stands, confirmed by Rav Yirmeya's observation.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.155b.4",
   "daf": "155b",
   "sentenceId": 3,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מַאי ״אֵין אוֹבְסִין״? אָמַר רַב יְהוּדָה: אֵין עוֹשִׂין לָהּ אֵבוּס בְּתוֹךְ מֵעֶיהָ. מִי אִיכָּא כִּי הַאי גַוְונָא? אִין, וְכִדְאָמַר רַב יִרְמְיָה מִדִּיפְתִּי: לְדִידִי חֲזֵי לִי הַהוּא טַיָּיעָא דְּאוֹכְלַהּ כּוֹרָא, וְאַטְעֵינַהּ כּוֹרָא.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» We learned in the mishna that one may not forcibly overfeed a camel on Shabbat. The Gemara asks: «What is» meaning of: «One may not forcibly overfeed? Rav Yehuda said: One may not» feed a camel to the point that «it creates a trough inside of its stomach.» The Gemara asks: «Is there» the possibility of feeding a camel «in that manner?» The Gemara answers: «Yes; and as Rav Yirmeya of Difti said: I saw» an «Arab who fed» his camel «a kor» of food «and loaded it» with another «kor» on its back."
  }
 ],
